{
  "id": "lowfield-cnp-thomas-pulse",
  "name": "Cnp Thomas pulse (patent table, 40 min)",
  "category": "lowfield",
  "evidence": "unverified",
  "fidelity": "timing",
  "dose": {"value": 400, "unit": "uT", "plane": "coil", "cite": "Thomas 2007 p.3 [251]"},
  "origin": "Lawson Health Research Institute / University of Western Ontario (A.W. Thomas, F.S. Prato); patent co-inventors M. Kavaliers and M.A. Persinger",
  "uT": 5,
  "summary": "The Lawson Cnp analgesia waveform, the decelerating 'Thomas pulse', from Table 1 of US patent 6,234,953 on all eight temporal coils, in the four-segment cycle the Lawson group describes, for the 40 min of the Thomas 2007 headset trial. At the coil and the scalp the field is close to the headset's (400 uT at its contact point); deep in the head it is 70 or more times weaker.",
  "notes": "Thomas 2007 ran a headset for 40 min and measured 400 uT and 800 mT/s 'at the contact point of the headset' (p.3 [251]); it gives no burst timing. Shupak 2006 states that its headset played the pulse of US 6,234,953, with earpieces over the temples, 'from above the temple to just above and behind the ear' (p.2 [86]). The Lawson group's papers give 853-ms segments of 18 pulses (Cook 2004 p.3 [198]; Cook 2005 p.3 [369]) with pauses of 110, 220, 330 and 1200 ms (Cook 2004 p.3 [198]). This program plays the patent's 1-ms point table, read column by column as the patent states: 18 identical pulses whose spacing grows from 28 to about 67 ms. The 18 whole pulses span 849 ms and each segment is filled with zero to 853 ms, which gives a 5272-ms cycle, about 455 cycles in 40 min. Camera 2015 state 838 ms per burst, which would cut the last pulse 11 ms short; that reading is not used. All eight temporal coils run in phase, left and right identical, in place of the two earpieces; FT7, T7, CP5, TP7 and their mirror on the right are this project's reading of the placement. Thomas 2007's +/-200 uT at the head surface and +/-35 uT deep in the brain describe the method in general (p.2 [250]), not the trial headset; Shupak 2006 gives 'a maximum of 200 uT (2 Gauss) to the deep brain' (p.2 [86]). This program's setting is the peak field 20 mm in front of each coil. At 5 uT the coil face gives 282 uT and the scalp gets 118 uT (477 and 200 uT at the helmet's maximum), close to the headset. Deep in the head it gives about 0.3 to 0.5 uT at 60 mm against +/-35 uT, 70 to 120 times less. The authors called Thomas 2007 essentially a negative study (fibromyalgia subgroup P = 0.06, n = 17). The human Cnp studies are small and from one lab, and the patent lists Persinger as a co-inventor, so this line is not independent of the God Helmet lab. Not a pain treatment: do not change medication or treatment. Not for people with epilepsy; stop on headache or distress.",
  "setting": "Seated or reclined in a quiet room (Thomas 2007 compared against identical, silent sham headsets).",
  "schedule": "Thomas 2007: 40 min twice a day, at least 4 h apart, for 7 days. Shupak 2004/2006: one 30-min session.",
  "targets": [
    "left-superior-temporal-gyrus-posterior",
    "right-superior-temporal-gyrus-posterior"
  ],
  "source": "US 6,234,953 B1 (Thomas, Prato, Kavaliers, Persinger), Table 1 https://patents.google.com/patent/US6234953B1/en ; Camera et al. 2015 (Cnp timing structure) https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4412122/ ; Thomas et al. 2007, Pain Res Manag https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2670735/ ; Shupak et al. 2004, Neurosci Lett https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15172106/ ; Shupak et al. 2006, Pain Res Manag https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16770449/",
